WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.380
We already discussed it here on VisualPolitik,
Argentina is a country where football is practically

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.680
a state religion... and where not a single
decade goes by without at least one major

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.880
economic crisis.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.400
For decades this country has been dominated
by a political model, a kind of ideology,

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.840
a political current, Peronism, which is something
like a mix of nationalism, protectionism,

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.310
and public spending.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:35.510
Subtitles: "Us, and when I talk about us,
I mean the political movement to which I belong,

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:36.550
yes, Peronism."

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:41.850
But in 2015 a new government arrived, that
of Mauricio Macri, with the mission of rebuilding

00:00:41.850 --> 00:01:00.469
the country from top to bottom.

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:05.789
In the 2017 legislative elections, the Argentine
people renewed their trust in this process.

00:01:05.789 --> 00:01:08.630
Basically, they came to say: Well, well done,
Macri!

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:17.700
So, the question that has arisen is, is Argentina
finally, once and for all, ready to take off?

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:25.540
So, let's see.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.740
On October 22nd, 2017, elections for Congress
and the Senate were held in Argentina.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:35.609
The government coalition, "Cambiemos", had
an… overwhelming triumph, with more than

00:01:35.609 --> 00:01:36.799
10 million votes.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:42.610
They won 15 out of the 24 electoral districts,
including the top 5, the most populated ones:

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:47.130
Buenos Aires Province, Buenos Aires City,
Córdoba, Santa Fé and Mendoza.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:52.159
Something like that hadn't happened since
1985...

00:01:52.159 --> 00:02:02.059
Now, after these elections, "Cambiemos" is
close to having an absolute majority, which

00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:05.760
ensures the government’s ability to push
their reform agenda forward.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:21.219
For 

00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:24.830
this reason, several analysts state that this
is Macri's terms true beginning.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:28.799
Now, after 2 years of power, what has Macri
done?

00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:29.799
What has he not done?

00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:31.209
And what are his intentions?

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:39.230
We will see this in this video, but first,
a bit of history.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:45.379
(DOWNFALL)

00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:50.030
A century ago, Argentina was one of the richest
countries in the world, even richer than countries

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:53.180
like Canada, Australia, France or even Germany.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.390
And not only that.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:00.299
When World War II ended on May 8, 1945, this
country was, along with the United States,

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:04.880
one of the largest creditors in the world
and also one of the largest gold owners on

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.519
the entire planet.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:11.969
At that time, Buenos Aires, the Argentine
capital, was in full swing and could even

00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:13.780
rival New York.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.810
But of course, having said that, you might
now be wondering: “What the hell happened

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.340
to Argentina?”

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.200
Well...

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:28.079
Even though things didn’t look bad for this
country after the Second World War, the truth

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:30.300
is that something was already changing...

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:37.340
The great international crisis of the 30s
affected this country a lot.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:43.099
Argentina, which was a huge agricultural exporter
suffered and had its first major financial

00:03:43.099 --> 00:03:44.150
crisis.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:48.469
Since then, nothing has been the same...

00:03:48.469 --> 00:03:55.379
Politics, dear viewer, began to intervene
in every economic and societal field.

00:03:55.379 --> 00:04:00.159
And finally in 1946, what we could call the
definitive turn began...

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:04.109
the government of General Farrell, first,
and then Colonel Juan Domingo Perón, approved

00:04:04.109 --> 00:04:09.670
all kinds of rules and laws, more than 16,000
in fact, which put the economy under state

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:14.580
control.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.250
Something which, for example, meant that things
like foreign trade, business creation or competition

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.109
wouldn’t be a priority.

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:26.900
Instead, policies such as import substitution,
nationalization, and large state enterprises,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:32.430
became the order of the day.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:37.650
This is how a 70-year downfall began, which
made Argentina become one of the few countries

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.520
that has gone from a developed country, to
a developing country...

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.000
Economic crisis, hyperinflation and payment
suspension have been very, very common problems

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.550
talked about in the Argentine media in recent
decades.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:05:02.139
And this process culminated with Kirchnerism
from Cristina Fernández de Kirchner.

00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:09.220
(HOW TO RUIN A COUNTRY)

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.400
Throughout her two terms as president, Cristina
Fernández de Kirchner set up a model that

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.060
could, simply, be defined as uneconomical.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.790
Yes, Argentina was already pretty terrible
at business...

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:21.080
but Cristina it took a step further.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:25.930
See, in “CFK’s” Argentina there were
capital controls, companies couldn't repatriate

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:30.210
their profits, taxes on companies reached
incomparable proportions in Latin America,

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:35.110
there were lots restrictions on imports, and
the Central Bank was only dedicated to making

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:42.280
money to finance the government.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:47.639
The model was so terribly uneconomic that
CFK set up a lot of taxes on exports, yep…

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:51.729
Who would think of punishing companies that
are exporting, selling to other countries?

00:05:51.729 --> 00:06:25.720
It’s pretty crazy...

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:29.539
But that isn’t it, with Cristina at the
helm of the Casa Rosada, the Argentine White

00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:33.930
House, this country became one of the most
protectionist in all of Latin America and

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:37.410
also into a country where setting up companies
was really difficult…

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.139
And of course, the results were immediate:

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:46.080
During her second term, growth was null, the
public deficit soared, inflation rose to 40%,

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.720
investment collapsed and suddenly, again,
nothing seemed to work.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:57.629
This is how, in December 2015, the Argentinian
people chose Mauricio Macri, former president

00:06:57.629 --> 00:07:05.849
of Boca Junior, as the new president of Argentina.

00:07:05.849 --> 00:07:17.130
(MACRI’S ARRIVAL)

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:31.050
Mauricio Macri’s first year leading Argentina
wasn’t easy.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:35.289
Of course, that was bound to happen because
of the terrible state he inherited the country

00:07:35.289 --> 00:07:36.289
in…

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:38.099
For example he reduced electricity and natural
gas subsidies.

00:07:38.099 --> 00:07:42.289
But, because reforms never have immediate
results, things didn’t look so great at

00:07:42.289 --> 00:07:46.389
the beginning.

00:07:46.389 --> 00:07:51.680
In 2016, the country suffered a severe recession
with a GDP drop of 2.6%; inflation approached

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.710
40%, poverty, unemployment, deficit and external
debt increased.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:59.710
In other words, there were ample opportunities
to criticize Macri...

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:03.699
I mean, wasn’t he the one that promised
loads of change?

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:10.080
Well, we’ve already said that reforms don’t
have immediate results, but in the end those

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:16.300
changes, they do arrive.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:19.539
In 2017, the Argentine economy was finally
showing signs of improvement.

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:24.500
This year's growth may exceed 3% and the appetite
for investment is already more than evident.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:33.470
Yes, that's right, it looks like Argentina
may be coming out of that long tunnel.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:43.200
For the time being, these results have given
Macri the great electoral victory we’ve

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.200
seen.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:46.650
Now, what exactly did this president do?

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:50.480
Well... he basically reduced all these, how
to say it… crazy and uneconomical regulations

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.030
and policies.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:55.590
The new government soon got to work...

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:57.920
there was no time to lose.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.730
As soon as he arrived, he eliminated currency
controls; allowed foreign investors to repatriate

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:08.480
their profits; eliminated the most incomprehensible
export taxes, and reduced subsidies and restrictions

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:09.690
on imports.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:15.450
All with one objective: Argentina had to recover
its productive economy.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:19.750
Also, and this is very important, Macri reached
agreements with some of the international

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:44.000
investors that Argentina hadn’t paid in
2001 and with whom it still had judicial fights;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.790
On the other hand, the new government approved
a kind of fiscal amnesty, which brought to

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:53.660
light, no more or less than $117 billion that
the Argentines had abroad and had not declared…

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.420
Before you think this is a big scandal, keep
reading.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:59.910
Think, what you would do if you were afraid
that your savings could be confiscated by

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:00.910
the government?

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.500
Sometimes it isn’t about paying less taxes...

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:08.200
When states don’t have legal security, it’s
easy for money to simply disappear.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:12.390
The fact is that, with all these measures,
Macri’s government managed to get the money

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:13.670
back to Argentina.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.640
And since then there has been an influx of
money that, for example, has triggered real

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:28.960
estate and car sales.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:40.060
It looks like the engine, quite literally,
has started again.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:53.090
But one question arises, and thats: “is
everything really is so... exemplary?

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:54.170
So perfect?”

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:58.300
Well hold on a moment, because the truth is
that until now the reforms have been very,

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:04.000
very gradual and with a fairly limited range;
something that in spite of the electoral results,

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:15.960
and all these calls to uncork the champagne,
has alerted several experts.

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:25.640
But, why so much distrust?

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:29.670
Because, despite the reforms we’ve seen,
it seems that this government is struggling

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:34.020
to overcome some of the typical problems of
Argentina’s previous governments.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:38.410
For example, public spending has not decreased,
it continues to rise and rise, which has caused,

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:42.920
among other things, foreign debt to soar by
more than $50 billion dollars since Macri

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:43.960
came to power.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:49.230
And that's not all: taxes are through the
roof; the central bank continues to finance

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.040
the government with new money; and according
to the latest Doing Business report, which

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:57.510
measures the ease for doing business in a
country, Argentina is still way way down in

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:59.760
116th place.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:07.710
Now, to be fair, we can say that Macri has
had two pretty-good "excuses":

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:12.040
First, is that not much time has passed; and
second, his political position in Congress

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:13.810
and the Senate were very weak.

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:20.590
Now, after these last elections, all that
is over...

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:28.010
(A REFORMIST AGENDA)

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:32.020
It seems that after his crushing electoral
triumph President Macri, has made it clear:

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:43.360
He has a 

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:46.060
task ahead of him which is somewhat... complicated.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:47.760
But what are his priorities?

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:53.750
Well fundamentally, dear viewer, at first
something that is as unpopular as it is necessary:

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:58.480
reducing public deficit and controlling inflation,
which are ultimately related.

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:03.000
Then get Argentina back to being a competitive
and attractive country to invest.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:07.450
To achieve this, it has promised to approve
a tax reform that reduces taxes on businesses,

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:12.680
a labor reform that facilitates hiring, and
more free trade agreements.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:20.160
Then, pension, educational and infrastructure
reforms are next.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:24.670
Yes, we can say that Macri has a lot of work
to do if he wants to meet the high expectations

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.330
that have been placed on him…

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:33.800
In any case, it seems that a new political
and economic era has begun for Argentina.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:39.040
A good example is Macri's commitment to reducing
poverty by formalizing and extending property

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:40.350
to the most disadvantaged.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:52.230
And 
by the way, we will be discussing how to fight

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:53.640
poverty in a future video.

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:54.640
So, you know, subscribe!

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:58.270
For now, the question is: Will this country
become great once again?

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:02.170
If they manage to deepen their reforms and
take advantage of all their opportunities,

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:06.200
such as the lithium industry, which we have
also discussed previously, I have little doubt

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:07.690
that this will be the case.

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:08.830
And now, what do you think?

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:12.020
Will Argentina become a great example for
the rest of Latin America?

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:15.230
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:19.500
I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did and don’t forget to

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:23.160
subscribe to our channel for brand new videos
every Monday and Thursday.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:28.410
Also, don’t forget to check out our friends
at the Reconsider Media Podcast - they provided

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.070
the vocals in this episode that were not mine!

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:41.280
And as always, thanks for watching!

